module runtime.abi_exception

/// **DO NOT IMPORT THIS INTO A PROGRAM YOU WILL RUN UNDER `pickle-cli run`
/// (the JIT).** On the AOT path (`pklc_build` / `pickle build`) it is safe and is
/// now the activation path. The warning this header used to carry was written
/// without distinguishing the two, so it was half right: correct for the JIT,
/// wrong for AOT. Details below; the JIT reason is the LAST section.
///
/// RE-MEASURED 2026-10-06, **on the AOT path**, four ways, all green:
///  1. Link gap vs an empty archive (`tools\diff\port\pklc_build.exe`): a probe
///     with `import { abiExcPending } from runtime.abi_exception` and NO call to
///     it resolves `pickle_exc_uncaught` — the symbol is absent from the
///     unresolved set entirely. The same probe with the import stripped reports
///     it unresolved. Baseline 4 unresolved vs 15 with the import (the import
///     also pulls `abi_string`, which surfaces `pickle_class_*`, `pickle_static_*`,
///     `pickle_stream_*`, `pickle_shadow_*`, `pickle_exit`).
///  2. Marker build against `combined.lib`: with a distinctive prefix inside
///     `abiExcUncaught`, the prefix PRINTS when the module is imported and does
///     NOT print when it is not. So the ported body both links and *beats* the
///     archive's Rust definition.
///  3. All 8 `tests/uncaught/*.pkl` fixtures, built with and without the import,
///     byte-compare stderr + exit: **8/8 IDENTICAL**, including
///     `uncaught_unicode_message.pkl` at 52 bytes (not the 76 of mojibake this
///     header used to warn about).
///  4. `tools\uncaught_parity.ps1` still reports **8/8** after the header rewrite.
///
///  5. **THE JIT IS DIFFERENT, AND THIS IS STILL BROKEN.** Same fixture,
///     `tests/uncaught/uncaught_basic.pkl` with one line added
///     (`import { abiExcPending } from runtime.abi_exception`), run under
///     `pickle-cli run`:
///       - no import: exit 1, 34 bytes of stderr = `uncaught exception: uncaught boom`
///       - + import:  **exit 0, no report at all**, stderr = 94 bytes of
///         `UNROOTED-ARG fn=abiExcThrow extern=pickle_obj_slot_set ...`
///     The warning *names `abiExcThrow`*, which proves the JIT executed the Pickle
///     body. The mechanism (recorded earlier, corroborated by this symptom): the
///     JIT rebinds `pickle_exc_throw` to the Pickle body, which writes the Pickle
///     class static `ExcChannel.inst.pending`, but the emitter's own
///     `pickle_exc_pending` check still resolves to the Rust function reading the
///     Rust `thread_local! PENDING`. The flag is written where nobody reads it, so
///     `take_escaped()` returns None and nothing is reported. AOT does not split,
///     because all three symbols are overridden together by `progOverrides` and
///     the entry point then calls Pickle end to end.
///     **A JIT cannot rebind an extern reference to a Pickle function for the
///     emitter's own check**, so as long as this module keeps its own channel while
///     Rust keeps `thread_local`, the two halves necessarily disagree under the
///     JIT. That — not DCE — is the real reason this file must stay *unimported*
///     for JIT-run programs, and the reason activation for the whole channel
///     belongs to an archive replacement rather than to an import.
///
/// WHY THE OLD CONCLUSION WAS TRUE WHEN WRITTEN (2026-10-03), AND WHY IT WAS
/// ALSO THE WRONG EXPLANATION. The activation mechanism is
/// `Emitter.overrides` (`pickle/compiler/emit.pkl`), the set of names the
/// emitter actually resolved from a `#[symbol("...")]` attribute on a
/// top-level callable; `finishLower` hands it to `linkExe` as
/// `progOverrides`; `linklib.pkl:307` then lets a program definition of an
/// overridden name win the duplicate against the archive. That
/// list was introduced 2026-10-04 (PORT-CHECKLIST "Duplicate-symbol resolution"),
/// one day AFTER the marker experiment — before it there was no way for a
/// program `#[symbol]` definition to displace an archive member, so the marker
/// could not print no matter what. The old text "a `#[symbol]` fn is only linked
/// if Pickle code actually calls it" was an inference from that marker result
/// under the pre-`progOverrides` linker. Calling is irrelevant.
///
/// The real rule, now measured on two independent clusters (`abi_fs`,
/// `abi_exception`) **on the AOT path**: a `#[symbol]` body is linked, and wins
/// over the archive, iff **(a) its module is imported and (b) codegen emits an
/// extern reference to that name**. Condition (b) is what `overrideSymbols` keys
/// on, so an import with no corresponding extern reference cannot activate
/// anything. Note what this rule is silent about: it says nothing about the two
/// sides of a channel agreeing once activated, which is the JIT failure in (5).
///
/// The second lesson stands: channels that share a payload representation must be
/// activated as a unit, and a half-activated channel fails *silently*. The
/// specific failure it was written for (Latin-1 mojibake on the unicode fixture
/// from a one-line `abi_string` import) no longer reproduces on AOT — see (3)
/// above — but it did happen, and (5) is a second, different half-activation that
/// does still reproduce.
///
/// Activation of the *whole* runtime still belongs to the port-runtime-objects
/// archive that replaces `combined.lib` — see PORT-CHECKLIST "ACTIVATION GAP".
/// Importing this module into a program means that program's exception channel is
/// the ported one **when that program is linked AOT**; when it is run under the
/// JIT, it means the channel splits and the program silently stops reporting
/// escaped throws.
import { byteAt } from runtime.abi_string
import { writeStderr } from runtime.abi_stdio
import { STR_BYTES_OFF } from runtime.layout

/// Word holding a string object's byte length. The header is
/// [class_id, size, len, bytes...], so the length sits at word 3 and the
/// inline payload starts at word 4 (`runtime/src/strings.rs`).
const LEN_WORD: int = 3

/// One `WriteFile` of a literal to stderr.
///
/// THE ROUTE IS THE REFERENCE'S. `exception.rs::report_escaped` writes through
/// `nostd::console_error_write`: `GetStdHandle(STD_ERROR_HANDLE)` plus one
/// `WriteFile`, no stream object and no flush - which is exactly what
/// `abi_console.pkl::emitCstr` does on the stdout handle. Going through
/// `stderr_stream()` here bought nothing and cost three extern references
/// (`stderr_stream`, `stream_write`, `stream_flush`), none of which the
/// reference itself uses on this path.
///
/// The write itself lives in `runtime.abi_stdio`, the architecture seam for
/// standard streams - see that file's header for why every stderr write is
/// routed through it.
fn writeErr(s: string) {
    unsafe {
        var p = &s
        let _ = writeStderr((p as int) + STR_BYTES_OFF, len(s))
    }
}

/// The same write for a string OBJECT - the throw payload is one. Word 3 of the
/// header is the byte length.
fn writeErrObj(obj: *int) {
    unsafe {
        let n = obj[LEN_WORD]
        if (n > 0) {
            let _ = writeStderr((obj as int) + STR_BYTES_OFF, n)
        }
    }
}

/// The in-flight throw channel, as two scalar globals.
///
/// WHY SCALARS, NOT A CLASS STATIC. This module's `pkl_setup_*` is injected
/// into the top of the compiled `main`, so a `class`-static singleton here
/// materialises through the emitter's normal static-initialiser lowering:
/// `pickle_class_new` (a 2-slot instance for `pending` + `payload`) plus
/// `pickle_box_bool(false)` to store `pending`. Native has no such object at
/// bring-up — its channel is `thread_local PENDING`/`PAYLOAD`, which allocates
/// nothing — so the reported GC counters (which count exactly
/// `Gc::alloc`/`abiRegisterObject` calls) would differ from the native
/// baseline by 2. Plain module-level `var` globals are image data with zero
/// defaults: the channel keeps the same single-process-wide ownership, costs
/// no allocations, and the counters match.
///
/// The shadow-stack ABI is the one that genuinely cannot make this trade,
/// because it must read the *previous* top-of-stack, which is per-thread by
/// definition; the exception channel has no such constraint (see the FILE
/// header's WHY THIS REPLACES TLS notes).
///
/// `excPayload` is deliberately never cleared to null: this language has no
/// null pointer literal (`0 as *int` is `E0363`), and the residual value is
/// unobservable (see the note on `abiExcTake`). Start at 0, which IS the null
/// pointer.
var excPending: int = 0
var excPayload: int = 0

/// ABI: publish `payload` and raise the pending flag.
///
/// Returns normally rather than branching away: the emitter places this on a
/// path that transfers control immediately afterwards, and a caller observes the
/// flag after the call.
///
/// A second throw while one is in flight replaces the payload, matching the
/// native ABI - the newer exception is the one that reaches the handler.
#[symbol("pickle_exc_throw")]
fn abiExcThrow(payload: *int) {
    excPayload = payload as int
    excPending = 1
}

/// ABI: is a throw in flight?
#[symbol("pickle_exc_pending")]
fn abiExcPending() -> bool {
    excPending == 1
}

/// ABI: consume the in-flight throw, returning its payload and clearing the flag.
///
/// The payload pointer is NOT reset to null afterwards, and that is a deliberate
/// divergence rather than an oversight. Native returns null here; this cannot,
/// because clearing the field would need the null pointer literal this language
/// does not have (a fresh null is only available from an unassigned field, not
/// an expression). It is also unobservable: `emit.pkl:12600` branches to a
/// handler on `pickle_exc_pending` and only then calls `pickle_exc_take` at
/// `:12618`, with no null test on the result, so `pending` alone is the validity
/// gate. The cost is that the last payload stays reachable from the channel
/// instead of being unrooted, which over-retains one real GC object rather than
/// leaking one.
#[symbol("pickle_exc_take")]
fn abiExcTake() -> *int {
    let payload = excPayload
    excPending = 0
    payload as *int
}

/// ABI: report a throw that escaped the program and exit 1, or return normally
/// when nothing escaped.
///
/// The backend emits `call pickle_exc_uncaught` on every return out of the
/// linked entry point (`x64.pkl:852-860`), placed before the frame teardown and
/// before the return value is loaded, so this runs on the way out of every
/// ported program. A program that threw nothing pays one field read.
///
/// The three writes mirror `exception.rs::report_escaped` exactly, and they must:
/// `tools/uncaught_parity.ps1:215` compares stderr BYTE-FOR-BYTE against the
/// native run. `uncaught exception: `, the message, `\n`.
///
/// Nothing here may `println`: that lowers to `pickle_str_concat` and re-enters
/// the body, which is the recursion trap documented in `abi_console.pkl`. The
/// `writeErr` helpers keep every extern off `pickle_print_obj` - and off the
/// stream symbols too, which is how this file stops referencing
/// `stderr_stream`/`stream_write`/`stream_flush`.
#[symbol("pickle_exc_uncaught")]
fn abiExcUncaught() {
    if (excPending == 0) {
        return
    }
    let payload = abiExcTake()
    writeErr("uncaught exception: ")
    writeErrObj(payload)
    writeErr("\n")
    exit(1)
}

/// The payload's message bytes, copied out of the string object's inline payload.
///
/// Reading the payload needs no null test, and that is measured rather than
/// assumed. `throw EXPR` only ever publishes a `string`: the checker has
/// established `Ty::Str`, and the option-wrap at `emit.pkl:12519` is a no-op for
/// strings - `option_wrap` lifts *scalars* into a boxed pointer
/// (`pickle_box_i64`/`f64`/`bool`/`char`) and returns every other type unchanged
/// (`_ => Ok(v)`). So the value handed to `pickle_exc_throw` is always a real
/// string object, and the native `if !payload.is_null()` guard in
/// `pickle_exc_throw` is defensive rather than load-bearing - the same finding
/// as the unreproduced `obj.is_null()` arm in `abi_console.pkl`.
fn payloadBytes(payload: *int) -> List<byte> {
    unsafe {
        let n = payload[LEN_WORD]
        var buf: List<byte> = bytes("")
        var i = 0
        while (i < n) {
            buf.push(byteAt(payload, i) as byte)
            i = i + 1
        }
        buf
    }
}
